### Why is newCachedThreadPool dangerous for long tasks?

The cached pool creates a new thread for every task that cannot be immediately handed off to an idle thread. With short tasks (milliseconds), threads complete quickly and are reused. With long tasks (seconds or minutes), no thread is ever free, so the pool grows without bound.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class CachedPoolDangerDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService dangerous = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
        for (int i = 0; i < 10_000; i++) {
            dangerous.submit(() -> {
                try { Thread.sleep(30_000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
                return "fraud check complete";
            });
        }
        // 10,000 threads created (none finished in time for reuse)
        // 10,000 * 1 MB stack = 10 GB of memory
        // OutOfMemoryError: unable to create new native thread
    }
}
```

### What is the safe alternative?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class SafeAlternativeDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ThreadPoolExecutor safe = new ThreadPoolExecutor(
                32, 32, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
                new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(1000),
                new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());
        // Bounded threads (32 max) + bounded queue (1000)
        // Under overload: CallerRunsPolicy slows the submitter
        // No thread explosion, no OOM
    }
}
```

### Why does blocking IO on the ForkJoin common pool cause thread explosion?

The common pool maintains a target parallelism of approximately cores minus 1. When a worker blocks through `ManagedBlocker`, the pool detects the reduction in active workers and creates a compensating thread. If the compensating thread also blocks, another is created, and so on. With many concurrent blocking calls, the compensation chain can create hundreds of threads.

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.stream.IntStream;

public class CommonPoolBlockingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // DANGEROUS: blocking HTTP inside parallel stream (common pool)
        IntStream.range(0, 100).parallel().forEach(i -> {
            try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
            // Common pool workers block → other parallel streams stall
        });
    }
}
```

### What is the fix for blocking work on ForkJoinPool?

```java
package com.bank.concurrency;

import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class DedicatedPoolForIODemo {
    private static final ExecutorService ioPool =
            new ThreadPoolExecutor(30, 30, 60L, TimeUnit.SECONDS,
                    new ArrayBlockingQueue<>(500),
                    new ThreadPoolExecutor.CallerRunsPolicy());

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Pass explicit IO pool to supplyAsync
        CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(
                () -> httpCall("http://api.bank.com/balance/SAV2001"),
                ioPool);  // NOT common pool!
        // Common pool free for CPU-bound parallel streams
    }

    static String httpCall(String url) {
        try { Thread.sleep(1000); } catch (InterruptedException e) { }
        return "75000.00";
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can you limit the common pool size? Yes, with `-Djava.util.concurrent.ForkJoinPool.common.parallelism=N`. But this is a tuning aid, not a fix for blocking IO.
- Is ManagedBlocker a reliable solution? For controlled, occasional blocking, yes. For bulk IO, no. A dedicated bounded pool is always the better design.
